00001| # Agent Evaluation Mode
00002| 
00003| **Measure agent performance, protocol compliance, and token efficiency.**
00004| 
00005| BeadsLog features a specialized **Evaluation Mode** designed to analyze how effectively AI agents use the knowledge graph versus brute-force searching. This provides quantitative proof of the benefits of the "Always-On Protocol."
00006| 
00007| ## 🚀 How it Works
00008| 
00009| Eval Mode attaches a unique **Session ID** to every command executed by an agent. This allows the system to segment continuous logs into distinct "test runs" for side-by-side comparison.
00010| 
00011| ### 🔄 The Eval Lifecycle
00012| 
00013| 1.  **Test:** `bd eval task`
00014|     - High-speed A/B/C testing using parallel execution (>5x speedup).
00015|     - Compares Implicit, Explicit, and Base (restricted) protocols.
00016|     - Features a real-time Bubble Tea progress UI and automated pre-hydration.
00017| 2.  **Start:** `bd eval start [scenario_id]`
00018|     - Activates high-fidelity tracing for manual agent runs.
00019|     - Sets the "Intent" or "Scenario" for grouping.
00020| 3.  **Iterate:** Run tasks with your agent.
00021| 4.  **Rotate:** `bd eval next`
00022|     - Ends the current run and starts a new one (rotates the Session ID).
00023|     - Perfect for A/B testing different models or prompts for the same task.
00024| 5.  **Report:** `bd eval report [N]`
00025|     - Generates a comparative matrix of the last N sessions, grouped by task similarity.
00026| 6.  **Stop:** `bd eval stop`
00027|     - Disables tracing and cleans up session state.
00028| 
00029| ## 📋 Methodology & Scoring
00030| 
00031| The reporting engine groups sessions by **Task Intent** using fuzzy matching (Levenshtein distance) on prompts. It then calculates a **Weighted Efficiency Score** based on the following rubric:
00032| 
00033| ### 1. Protocol Adherence Rubric
00034| 
00035| | State | Logic | Emoji | weight |
00036| | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
00037| | **Optimal (Mastery)** | `Map` tools used FIRST + `Verify` tools used LATER | 🟢🟢🟢 | **1.5x** |
00038| | **Shallow** | `Map` tools used + NO `Verify` tools (hallucination risk) | 🟠 | **0.8x** |
00039| | **Disordered** | `Verify` tools used BEFORE `Map` tools (sequence break) | 🔴🟠 | **0.5x** |
00040| | **Blind** | NO `Map` tools used (pure brute-force) | 🔴 | **0.1x** |
00041| 
00042| ### 2. Efficiency Formula
00043| 
00044| Efficiency is calculated relative to the average token cost of all runs in the same task group:
00045| 
00046| `Efficiency = (Strategy Weight) * (Group Average Tokens / This Run Tokens)`
00047| 
00048| - **> 1.0x 🚀:** More efficient than the group average.
00049| - **< 1.0x 📉:** Less efficient (wasted tokens or poor strategy).
00050| 
00051| ### 💰 Token Weighting
00052| 
00053| To estimate costs accurately, tools are assigned weights based on their context consumption:
00054| - **Brute-Force (`grep`, `search_file_content`):** ~2,500 tokens (Heavy context).
00055| - **Inspection (`read_file`):** ~1,200 tokens (Medium context).
00056| - **Retrieval (`bd devlog search/graph`):** ~150 tokens (Light context).
00057| 
00058| ## 📊 Comparative Report Example
00059| 
00060| When you run `bd eval report 3`, you get a grouped matrix:
00061| 
00062| ```text
00063| TASK GROUP: "Authentication Logic Fix" (Matched 2 runs)
00064| ╭───────────────────┬───────────────────┬───────────────────┬──────────────────┬──────────────────┬──────────────────┬──────────────────╮
00065| │ Run               │ Strategy          │ Tools Used        │ Tokens           │ Time             │ Status           │ Eff.             │
00066| ├───────────────────┼───────────────────┼───────────────────┼──────────────────┼──────────────────┼──────────────────┼──────────────────┤
00067| │         1         │  Optimal (Mastery)│  bd devlog graph  │       1350       │        12s       │       PASS       │     2.5x 🟢🟢🟢  │
00068| │ (770496985-61285) │                   │     read_file     │                  │                  │    (100/100)     │                  │
00069| ├───────────────────┼───────────────────┼───────────────────┼──────────────────┼──────────────...
In this project, **Scoring** refers to the evaluation rubric used to measure AI agent performance, protocol compliance, and token efficiency. It is defined in two ways:

### 1. Functional Definition (Methodology)
As documented in `docs/EVAL.md`, scoring is based on a **Protocol Adherence Rubric** and an **Efficiency Formula**:

| State | Logic | Emoji | Weight |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Optimal (Mastery)** | `Map` tools used FIRST + `Verify` tools used LATER | 🟢🟢🟢 | **1.5x** |
| **Shallow** | `Map` tools used + NO `Verify` tools | 🟠 | **0.8x** |
| **Disordered** | `Verify` tools used BEFORE `Map` tools | 🔴🟠 | **0.5x** |
| **Blind** | NO `Map` tools used (pure brute-force) | 🔴 | **0.1x** |

**Efficiency Formula:**
`Efficiency = (Strategy Weight) * (Group Average Tokens / This Run Tokens)`

---

### 2. Code Definition (Data Structure)
In the implementation (`cmd/bd/eval_task_opencode.go`), `Scoring` is defined as a Go struct:

```go
type Scoring struct {
    Strategy   string  // e.g., "Optimal", "Blind"
    LogicDesc  string  // e.g., "Map FIRST + Verify LATER"
    Status     string  // "PASS" or "FAIL"
    Bonus      float64 // Numerical weight/bonus
    Emoji      string  // Visual indicator (🟢, 🟠, 🔴)
}
```

This struct is primarily used by the `bd eval task` and `bd eval report` commands to categorize and display the performance of different agent runs.